  "query_preview": "..\n\nQuiet conditions persist across Southeast Michigan this afternoon on \nthe western flank of departing surface high pressure. Thicker low-\nlevel cloud remains in tact across the Saginaw Valley and Thumb, \nwhere temperatures are running several degrees cooler (mid 30s) than \nthe Detroit Metro area (mid 40s) where static stability aloft \ndominates. \n\nAttention turns to a complex interaction of PV anomalies for the \nThanksgiving holiday into Friday. Current water vapor imagery shows \ntwo distinct PV anomalies: one digging across the central Plains and \nanother wave near the Gulf. These disturbances will phase between \nnow and Thursday, carving out a deep closed upper low over the \nTennessee Valley and inducing cyclogenesis along the mid-Atlantic \ncoast that evolves into an elongated surface trough across the Ohio \nValley by Thursday night. The low will gradually retrograde toward \nthe main height fall center over the Ohio Valley, drawing a feed of \nAtlantic moisture into the Great Lakes. This establishes a prolonged \nperiod of deep moist isentropic ascent and thus wet conditions across\nthe Great Lakes to end the holiday week. \n\nPrecipitation expands north into SE Michigan late Thanksgiving \nmorning into the afternoon. With a deep layer of low level dry air \nin place, initial precipitation type could be a mix of rain and wet \nsnowflakes due to wet bulbing, followed by a transition to all rain \nas the warm nose strengthens. There is potential for frontogenetic \ninfluence within the broader precipitation shield which could \nproduce a period of more intense hourly rainfall rates (around \n0.1\"/hr) as the lead edge lifts through. This would occur alongside \na broader area of ascent from the synoptic low and diabatic cooling \ndue to evaporation/melting snowflakes, which works to erode the warm\nlayer. Model guidance is picking up on this cooling signature, with \nthe column cooling below 0 C everywhere but the lowest 1-2 kft by \nThursday night. These WBZ heights mean model consensus is still for \nmostly rain with some larger wet snowflakes able to mix in overnight,\nalthough it is shallow enough to keep a close eye on p-type Thursday\nnight-Friday especially for higher elevation areas like the Irish \nHills. Should note the ensemble envelope has seen an increase in\nnumber of members producing snow, although assumed 10:1 ratios are \nlikely overdoing it on accumulation potential. The cooling process \nalso increases mixing depths to tap into a strong LLJ with 45-50 \nknots of NE flow in the lowest 5 kft. Breezy NE flow could gust 35 to\n40 mph late Thursday into the overnight. \n\nClassic TROWAL signature then moves overhead during the day Friday, \nproducing a final flare up of rain/snow across the cwa before ending \nFriday evening. Regardless of p-type storm total liquid will \napproach or potentially exceed an inch, which will be our heaviest \nprecipitation event this month by far. Ridging brings a brief lull \nSaturday before a more progressive wave tracks across the Ohio \nValley Sunday. Models attm still on the warm side for this system \nfor a mostly rain event.",
